# Smart Farm DSS — Environment Variables
# ──────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file:  cp .env.example backend/.env
# Ask a teammate for the DATABASE_URL connection string.
# Only DATABASE_URL and JWT_SECRET are required to run the app.

# ============================================
# APPLICATION
# ============================================
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3001
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3001
UPLOAD_DIR=uploads

# ============================================
# DATABASE (required)
# Ask a teammate for the shared Atlas URL, or use local MongoDB.
# See README.md → "Database" section for setup instructions.
# ============================================
DATABASE_URL=<ask a teammate>

# ============================================
# AUTHENTICATION (required)
# ============================================
# Signs every access and refresh token: anyone who knows this value can mint a
# token for any account. So there is no default here, not even a development
# one — a working value in a committed example file is a value that reaches
# production, because it never fails loudly enough to get replaced.
#
# Generate your own:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Must be at least 32 characters; the backend checks at boot and exits if not.
JWT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_A_RANDOM_STRING_OF_AT_LEAST_32_CHARACTERS
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d
BCRYPT_ROUNDS=10

# ============================================
# WEATHER — no key needed
# Open-Meteo is used for weather/irrigation. No API key required.
# ============================================
# OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=

# ============================================
# CROP PHOTO ANALYSIS  ← set GEMINI_API_KEY, this is the one that matters
#
# Without at least one engine below, an uploaded photo is stored but never
# looked at: the diagnosis falls back to the farmer's typed description and the
# weather record alone. That is a much weaker answer, and it is what happens by
# default, so it is worth thirty seconds to set this.
#
# 1) Google Gemini — recommended. Free, no billing account, no credit card:
#      https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#    Sign in, "Create API key", paste it below. Free-tier request limits are far
#    above what one farm's uploads will reach.
# ============================================
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_VISION_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# GEMINI_TIMEOUT_MS=45000

# 2) Local Ollama model — free and private, but needs the model pulled onto this
#    machine (~3 GB) and is less accurate than Gemini:
#      ollama pull gemma3:4b
#    Used as the fallback when Gemini is unset or unreachable. Set
#    VISION_PREFER_LOCAL=true to put it *ahead* of Gemini, for deployments where
#    crop photographs must not leave the premises.
# OLLAMA_VISION_MODEL=gemma3:4b
# OLLAMA_VISION_ENABLED=true
# VISION_PREFER_LOCAL=false

# 3) Plant.id — purpose-built for plant disease and returns reference photos,
#    but the key is paid. Tried last.
# PLANT_ID_API_KEY=

# ============================================
# MARKET PRICES (optional)
# Free key from https://data.gov.in/
# ============================================
# DATA_GOV_IN_API_KEY=

# ============================================
# GOOGLE SIGN-IN (optional)
# ============================================
# "Continue with Google" on the sign-in and sign-up screens. Unset simply hides
# the button; username/email + password always works.
#
# Get one at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application
#   Authorised JavaScript origins:  http://localhost:3000
#                                   (plus your deployed frontend URL)
#   Authorised redirect URIs:       none needed — see below
#
# We verify the ID token the browser receives instead of running a server-side
# code exchange, so there is NO client secret in this project. Do not add one.
# The same client id goes in the frontend as NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID; it is
# public by design and ships in the browser bundle.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ============================================
# EMAIL OTP — Gmail SMTP (optional)
# ============================================
# Delivers one-time codes through smtp.gmail.com. Leave both unset and OTP
# email is simply disabled — the server still boots and every other feature
# works, matching how the keys above degrade.
#
# EMAIL_APP_PASSWORD must be a Google *App Password*, not the Gmail account
# password. Gmail has refused plain-password SMTP since May 2022.
#   1. Turn on 2-Step Verification: https://myaccount.google.com/security
#   2. Create one at https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#   3. Google displays 16 characters in four groups ("abcd efgh ijkl mnop").
#      The spaces are presentation only — store the 16 characters unbroken.
#
# Treat this value like a password: it grants send-as access to the mailbox and
# bypasses 2FA. Revoke it from the same page if it ever leaks.
# EMAIL_USER=your-account@gmail.com
# EMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=abcdefghijklmnop

# ============================================
# FRONTEND
# ============================================
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3001/api

# The same value as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above. Public by design — anything prefixed
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is compiled into the browser bundle. Unset hides the button.
# See frontend/.env.local.example, which is where the frontend actually reads it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
